{0 Queries}

{!Eon_ecs.Query} iterates entities that own a chosen intersection of
components. It never walks the whole world — it always starts from the
smallest of the requested sparse sets, so a query over a rare component is
cheap regardless of how many total entities exist. Examples below are from
the companion
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_ecs/examples/book/queries_example.ml}[queries_example.ml]},
reusing the {{!page-world_and_components}[Position]/[Velocity]/[Health]}
component modules from the previous chapter.

{1 iter1 through iter4}

Fixed-arity iterators — pick the one matching how many components your
system needs simultaneously:

{[
  Query.iter1 world Position.name (fun _entity (_pos : Position.t) -> ())
]}

{[
  (* iter2: entities that have BOTH named components *)
  Query.iter2 world Position.name Velocity.name
    (fun entity (pos : Position.t) (vel : Velocity.t) ->
       ignore (entity, pos.x +. vel.dx, pos.y +. vel.dy))
]}

An entity missing even one of the requested components is silently
excluded — a [Position]-only entity never reaches an [iter2] callback that
also asks for [Velocity]. [iter3] and [iter4] follow the same shape for
three and four components. There is no [iter5] — past four components, use
{!Eon_ecs.Query.iter_entities} (below) with a name list instead.

{1 Smallest-set-first}

[Query.count] and [Query.iter_entities] generalize past a fixed arity by
taking a component-name list, and they share the same core algorithm:
{b pick the smallest of the requested sparse sets as the iteration base},
then check the remaining names as a membership filter. The base set's size
— not the total entity count in the world — drives the cost:

{[
  (* 6 entities have Position; only 1 has Health. Health becomes the base:
     Query.count walks that ONE entity and checks it against Position,
     never scanning the other 5 Position-only entities. *)
  let world = make_world () in
  for i = 1 to 6 do
    let e = World.create_entity world in
    World.add_component world e ~name:Position.name { Position.x = float_of_int i; y = 0.0 };
    if i = 3 then
      World.add_component world e ~name:Health.name { Health.current = 100; max = 100 }
  done;
  assert (Query.count world [ Position.name; Health.name ] = 1)
]}

This is exactly why a query over a rare component (a boss marker, a
one-off status effect) stays cheap even in a world with thousands of common
components — the rare component's own sparse set is small, and it's always
what gets picked as the base.

{1 A subtlety: two different exceptions for the same mistake}

Both [Query.count] and [Query.iter_entities] raise if any requested name
was never registered:

{[
  match Query.count world [ "Never_registered" ] with
  | _ -> assert false
  | exception Invalid_argument _ -> ()
]}

Note the exception type: {b [Invalid_argument]}. This is worth calling out
explicitly because it's easy to assume it matches
{{!page-world_and_components}[World.get_component]}'s behavior from the
previous chapter — but [World.get_component]/[add_component]/
[set_component]/[remove_component] raise {b [Failure]} for the identical
underlying mistake (an unregistered component name). Both exceptions mean
the same thing — a missing {!Eon_ecs.World.register_component} call — but
if you're writing a [try ... with] around ECS calls that might touch either
surface, catch both, not just one.

